      "math_academy_id": "q-175390",
      "sequence_position": 14,
      "problem": "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $x+y\\le 0,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?",
      "worked_solution": "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $x+y\\le 0$\n\nNow, we convert the inequality into the slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\le 0 \\\\ y & \\le -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253115/assets/1e8f03a8b229a8b2e46af33c700dec57a8abed693a58c75b6312e2ae5a6856c1.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
